#[doc("""
The document as a value: the intermediate form between source text and
ANSI lines. Pure and effect-free — derived `Eq` and `Show` are what let
the parser be tested by structural equality.
""")]

#[doc("""
Content within a line. `Text` is the literal, already stripped of its
markup. The block phase produces only `Text`; the inline scanner
replaces those nodes with the tree of emphasis, code and links.

`Break` is a hard line break — the two trailing spaces or the trailing
backslash the source used to insist on a break the wrap would not have
chosen. A soft newline is a space and leaves no node.

`Ref` is the unresolved half of a reference link, `[text][label]`, and
never survives `parser.parse`: the definitions are gathered from the
whole document first, so the resolution happens once the last one has
been seen. What no definition matches degrades to its literal text.
`ImageRef` is the same for `![alt][label]`.

`Image` carries the alt text and the source path. It is the markup, not
the picture: the bytes are read at the edge, and an image the terminal
can actually draw becomes a `Picture` block before the renderer sees
it.
""")]
#[derive(Eq, Show)]
pub type Inline
  = Text(String)
  | Emph([Inline])
  | Strong([Inline])
  | Strike([Inline])
  | CodeSpan(String)
  | Link([Inline], String)
  | Image([Inline], String)
  | Ref([Inline], String)
  | ImageRef([Inline], String)
  | Break

#[doc("""
How a column is aligned, as its delimiter row declared it: `:--` left,
`--:` right, `:-:` centre, and a bare `---` left, the default.
""")]
#[derive(Eq, Show)]
pub type Align = AlignLeft | AlignCenter | AlignRight

#[doc("""
One entry of a list: the blocks that make up its content, and whether
the source opened it with a task box. `task` is `None` for an ordinary
item, `Some(false)` for `- [ ]` and `Some(true)` for `- [x]` — a list
may mix the two, so the box belongs to the item and not to the list.
""")]
#[derive(Eq, Show)]
pub type Item = { task: Option[Bool], blocks: [Block] }

#[doc("""
A top-level block. `Code` carries the fence language (empty when none
was declared) and its raw lines, never reinterpreted. `Bullets` and
`Ordered` hold each item as an `Item`, whose blocks are what allows
paragraphs and sublists to nest inside it. `Ordered` remembers the
number the list started at.

`Picture` is an image the terminal agreed to draw: the escape sequence
already built, and the alt text it falls back to. Nothing produces one
until `main` has read the file and asked the terminal, so the parser
never emits it and the pure pipeline never reads a byte from disk.

`Table` holds the header row, one alignment per column, and the body
rows — every row already cut to the header's number of columns, so the
renderer never has to ask whether a row is ragged. A cell is `[Inline]`
like any other text: emphasis and links work inside one.
""")]
#[derive(Eq, Show)]
pub type Block
  = Heading(Int, [Inline])
  | Para([Inline])
  | Code(String, [String])
  | Quote([Block])
  | Bullets([Item])
  | Ordered(Int, [Item])
  | Table([[Inline]], [Align], [[[Inline]]])
  | Picture(String, [Inline])
  | Rule
